.TH S3M-SYNC 1 "" "s3m @VERSION@" "s3m Manual"
.SH NAME
s3m\-sync \- parallel synchroniser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3m\-sync
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fISRC\fR \fIDST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Synchronises SRC to DST, copying only what is new or changed.
SRC and DST are a local directory or an \fBs3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR URI:
.TP
local directory to s3://
upload
.TP
s3:// to local directory
download
.TP
s3:// to s3://
server-side copy (same endpoint)
.TP
local directory to local directory
parallel local mirror (atomic copies, source mtimes preserved)
.PP
By default this is a \fBdry run\fR: the plan is printed as CSV and nothing is changed.
Add \fB\-\-apply\fR to synchronise.
.PP
The run has three phases: index the destination (parallel listing or local walk), diff the source against the index to build the plan, then execute the plan across the worker pool.
An entry is copied when it is missing from the destination, its size differs, or the source is newer (unless \fB\-\-size\-only\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Actually copy and delete.
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Also remove destination entries that do not exist in the source.
If indexing the destination hit errors, the delete pass is skipped entirely and copies still proceed.
.TP
.B \-\-size\-only
Compare by size alone; ignore timestamps.
.TP
.B \-\-checksum
Compare content: local MD5 against the object etag where the etag is conclusive (single-part uploads), falling back to size and mtime for multipart etags.
S3 to S3 compares etags directly; local to local hashes both files.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-threads " N"
Worker threads, 1\-256 (default 16).
.TP
.BI \-\-shard\-depth " N"
Prefix levels expanded for parallelism, 0\-9 (default 2).
.TP
.B \-\-rrdns
Resolve the endpoint hostname to every A/AAAA address and spread worker threads across them, moving a thread to the next address if its current one fails.
See
.BR s3m (7).
.TP
.B \-\-s3fs\-meta
On uploads, store each file's mode, mtime, uid and gid as the \fBx\-amz\-meta\-*\fR attributes s3fs reads, so the objects look native when the bucket is mounted with s3fs.
Without it s3fs falls back to default permissions and the upload time as mtime.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " FILE"
Write the CSV plan or report to FILE and show a live progress display.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the console listing; progress and the summary are still shown.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH TRANSFERS
Uploads stream from disk with a Content-Type guessed from the file extension.
Files over 128 MiB use multipart upload, aborted server-side on failure.
Downloads write a temporary file next to the target and rename it into place, with mtime set to the object's LastModified.
Server-side copies move no data through the client; objects over 5 GiB use multipart UploadPartCopy.
Symbolic links are never followed or synced.
.SH OUTPUT
CSV with the columns \fBkey,action,size,result\fR.
\fBaction\fR is \fBupload\fR, \fBdownload\fR, \fBcopy\fR, \fBdelete\fR or \fBdelete\-local\fR; \fBresult\fR is \fBpending\fR (dry run), \fBdone\fR or \fBfailed: REASON\fR.
.SH CONNECTION OPTIONS
The connection options \fB\-\-endpoint\fR, \fB\-\-region\fR, \fB\-\-profile\fR, \fB\-\-path\-style\fR, \fB\-\-no\-sign\fR, \fB\-\-insecure\fR and \fB\-\-ca\-bundle\fR are common to all s3m tools and are described in
.BR s3m (7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success; 1 completed with errors; 2 usage, startup or safety-guard refusal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dry run of an upload:
.PP
.RS
.nf
s3m\-sync ./website s3://prod\-site/static
.fi
.RE
.PP
Publish, removing files deleted locally, with an audit CSV:
.PP
.RS
.nf
s3m\-sync \-\-apply \-\-delete \-o publish.csv ./website s3://prod\-site/static
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restore a prefix to disk:
.PP
.RS
.nf
s3m\-sync \-\-apply s3://backup/data/reports ./reports
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR s3m (7)
.BR s3m\-cp (1),
.BR s3m\-diff (1)
